+    /**
+     * True when the target is a Windows DEVICE path ({@code \\.\...} or {@code \\?\...})
+     * rather than a regular file - e.g. the usbprint path
+     * {@code \\.\USB#VID_xxxx&PID_yyyy#instance#{GUID}\dummy} used for direct USB
+     * receipt printing.
+     * <p>
+     * REGRESSION FIXED HERE (measured in production, albasushilab 2026-09-02): patch 0006
+     * swapped FileOutputStream for FileChannel.open(WRITE, CREATE, TRUNCATE_EXISTING),
+     * claiming those were the "same open flags as FileOutputStream". They are NOT, and on
+     * Windows two of them are illegal on a device:
+     * <ul>
+     *   <li>CREATE maps to CreateFile(OPEN_ALWAYS) - a device cannot be created;</li>
+     *   <li>TRUNCATE_EXISTING issues SetEndOfFile, which the usbprint driver does not
+     *       implement, returning ERROR_INVALID_FUNCTION (1).</li>
+     * </ul>
+     * Java surfaces that as {@code java.nio.file.FileSystemException: ...\dummy:
+     * Funcion incorrecta} - i.e. EVERY direct-USB receipt failed, on every Windows till,
+     * from the moment patch 0006 shipped. It went unnoticed because these flags are
+     * harmless on Linux (the kernel ignores O_TRUNC on a character device), which is the
+     * only platform patch 0006's own test covers.
+     * <p>
+     * The distinction is the TARGET, not the OS: only a real file can be created or
+     * truncated. Devices are opened WRITE-only, exactly as FileOutputStream did before.
+     */
+    static boolean isDevicePath(File file) {
+        if(file == null) { return false; }
+        String p = file.getPath();
+        return p.startsWith("\\\\.\\") || p.startsWith("\\\\?\\");
+    }
